/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050271 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1033 3187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050355 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429565 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Sinusoidal Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1033 6680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1034 0.9905710772730931 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050392 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "45561e84-38ff-5ce1-9f3c-76fdda09730b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186477830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e2d776b-916a-506e-bc6f-9af1b018f8f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157051 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157051 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157051 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157052 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157052 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/db/db5806ea651ef0c5a635e5b019ad77c1dc4f93c3f8d7d0a9ae525669ff25c776.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](images/0c/0c0a62974ee1a4d6b4f35b49f36d737cdb19a2190983bb28b55a74e01daae4d6.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/55/55ecfbb20bb6e0166c9d676d9290c15c795ec855e2f171bc21b9df93c4bc497f.svg)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157052 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157053 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157053 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/51/51f35485ec1b324b13c8d0a6e80b3c09f655f985a56ba9c5583cedb9fd4d2168.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](images/18/1885f67835b63059d8212b9d96f4a4b916c01205d0be9043722e1e16aeb06e64.svg).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/c3/c3d19c5c31140ed5bb678da9defeab18bbaf712453735d147b3d5063f9137363.svg)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157053 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157455 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1759